  "enrichment": {
    "search_order_mechanism": "WmiPrvSE.exe runs from C:\\Windows\\System32\\wbem\\. Windows resolves a DLL dependency from the executable's own directory before falling back to System32. The legitimate wbemcomn.dll ships in C:\\Windows\\System32\\; nothing ships at C:\\Windows\\System32\\wbem\\wbemcomn.dll. Planting a file at that path therefore wins the search order for every WmiPrvSE instance on the host.",
    "signature_contrast": "Two loads of the same file name appear in this window. C:\\Windows\\System32\\wbemcomn.dll is Signed=true, Signature='Microsoft Windows' \u2014 the genuine module. C:\\Windows\\System32\\wbem\\wbemcomn.dll is Signed=false \u2014 the planted one. Path, not file name, distinguishes them.",
    "delivery_path": "The planted DLL arrived over SMB, not from a local process: Security 5145 records writes to \\\\*\\C$ with RelativeTargetName 'Windows\\System32\\wbem\\wbemcomn.dll' by CORP\\j.marsh, and the corresponding Sysmon FileCreate on WKS-4472 is attributed to Image 'System' PID 4 \u2014 the kernel SMB server, which is how remote share writes always appear locally.",
    "access_mask_decode": {
      "0x17019f": "Write-heavy access including WriteData, AppendData, WriteEA and WriteAttributes \u2014 a file being created or overwritten, not read.",
      "0x80": "ReadAttributes \u2014 a directory or file being stat-ed."
    },
    "trigger_mechanism": "The load did not happen when the file was written. It happened ~31 seconds later, when 'wmic /node:WKS-4472 os get' was run from WKS-4471. That remote WMI query caused DCOM on WKS-4472 to spawn a fresh WmiPrvSE.exe, which loaded the planted DLL during initialisation. The attacker controls when the payload runs by choosing when to query.",
    "payload_evidence": "0.013 seconds after the unsigned load, WmiPrvSE.exe spawned notepad.exe running as NT AUTHORITY\\NETWORK SERVICE. WmiPrvSE has no legitimate reason to launch an interactive editor. In this capture notepad is a benign stand-in for arbitrary code; the security-relevant fact is that the hijacked provider achieved child-process execution under a service identity.",
    "asset_note": "The same technique executed against DC01 would run under the domain controller's provider host. WmiPrvSE instances on DC01 appear in this window loading only the legitimate System32 copy.",
    "unsigned_is_not_sufficient": "Signed=false alone does not indicate compromise. This same window contains 37 other unsigned module loads, all on DC01, all from C:\\Windows\\assembly\\NativeImages_v4.0.30319_64\\. Those are NGEN native images \u2014 ahead-of-time compiled .NET assemblies generated locally by the machine itself, which carry no Authenticode signature by design and are entirely routine. What separates the alerting load is not that it is unsigned, but that an unsigned file appeared at a path that shadows a genuine system DLL, arrived from a remote host minutes earlier, and was followed by child-process execution."
  }
